{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem.\n\n*Early Life*\nRaymond III was born in 1140 to Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His father was the Count of Tripoli and his mother was the sister of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. Raymond III's early life is not well documented but it is known that he succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152.\n\nRaymond III's reign was marked by conflicts with the neighboring Muslim states. In 1152 he led a campaign against the Fatimid Caliphate but was defeated. He also clashed with the Zengid dynasty which controlled much of the surrounding territory. Despite these conflicts Raymond III was able to maintain control of Tripoli and even expanded its borders.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin*\nIn 1187 Raymond III led a contingent of troops from Tripoli to join the army of King Guy of Jerusalem in the Battle of Hattin. The battle was a disaster for the Crusaders and Raymond III was among those who fled the battlefield. He was criticized for his role in the defeat and was accused of cowardice.\n\n*Later Life and Death*\nAfter the Battle of Hattin Raymond III returned to Tripoli and attempted to rebuild his army. However he died in 1187 before he could launch any new campaigns. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli.\n\nRaymond III's legacy is complex. He was a skilled military leader but his defeats at the hands of the Muslims weakened the Crusader states. Despite this he is remembered as one of the most important figures of the 12th century Crusades.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004263356793671846}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.318112355074845e-06}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.25955715775489807}, {"token": " of